// # fs/os — Operating System Surface
//
// Logline: The OS syscall shims (sys_exec, sys_args, sys_prctl), the policy getenv filter (OODA_/OO_ keys only), the file I/O surface, the net-floor loopback constraint, the host process info, the rlimit setter, the time+rand source, and the Landlock-gated proc_mem.
//
// Beats:
//   1. sys.c — orchestrator: sys_exec, sys_args, policy getenv, child filter env.
//   2. sys_env.c — policy getenv filter (OODA_/OO_ keys only).
//   3. sys_stamp.c — file mtime stamp (oo_file_stamp, v3.0.0 cap-gated).
//   4. sys_stdin.c — stdin read (oo_read_stdin / oo_read_stdin_chunk, v3.0.0 cap-gated).
//   5. fs.c — orchestrator: file I/O with cap-gated path safety.
//   6. fs_dir.c — directory iteration (oo_fs_dir / oo_fs_dir_next).
//   7. fs_lowlevel.c — open/read/write/close with cap-gated path validation.
//   8. netfloor.c — orchestrator: net-floor loopback constraint.
//   9. netfloor_tcp.c — TCP send/recv on the loopback floor.
//  10. netfloor_udp.c — UDP send/recv on the loopback floor.
//  11. host.c — host process info (pid, ppid, etc.).
//  12. dns.c — the loopback-only resolver.
//  13. rlimit.c — setrlimit wrapper.
//  14. time_rand.c — monotonic time + secure random.
//
// v2.3.0 file split: the original 279-line fs.c, 293-line netfloor.c,
// and 306-line sys.c were each split into an orchestrator +
// 2-3 satellite files. The split is functional (one syscall family
// per file): low-level vs. dir, TCP vs. UDP, env vs. stamp vs. stdin.
//
// ──▓▓ fs/os ANCHOR ──▓▓──
// :: fail-closed: no getaddrinfo, no unrestricted env ::
